{
  "image_name": "0fd82b35d2d1d0d1584af59882c94ea8.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress links damaging stimuli to cell stress responses. At the top, biomechanical injury, inflammation, and oxidative stress are shown as upstream triggers that cause ER stress and accumulation of unfolded proteins in the ER lumen. These abnormal proteins appear to draw the chaperone GRP78 away from three ER membrane stress sensors—PERK, IRE1α, and ATF6—thereby activating them.\n\nThe diagram highlights three parallel signaling branches of the unfolded protein response. PERK signals through phosphorylated eIF2α and CHOP, with IL-1β noted downstream, leading to increased oxidative stress, pro-catabolic responses, and apoptosis. IRE1α promotes alternative splicing of XBP1 mRNA, converting unspliced XBP1 into active XBP1s, which is associated with increased pro-catabolic responses. ATF6 is shown moving to the Golgi, where it is cleaved into an active form, p50, and this pathway appears to increase XBP1 expression, feeding into the XBP1 branch.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that ER stress acts as a central hub translating injury and inflammatory cues into degradative, stress-related, and cell death pathways.",
